{
  "_comment": "@shexjs/neighborhood-sparql's manifest vocabulary: what an examples manifest may write inside the mapping it binds to this file -- the backend's own parameters, its module's dbParams: endpoint (the one doc/tests-manifest-ld.yaml exercises) and allOutgoing. A manifest binds this file under a term of its own choosing and gives that term this file's URL as its property-scoped @context (JSON-LD 1.1): in the manifest's @context, `nsp: http://shex.js.org/neighborhoods/Sparql/#` and, beside it, `nsp:parms: {@context: ../packages/neighborhood-sparql/manifest-context.jsonld}`; an entry then writes `nsp:parms:` with these names bare inside it, plus pluginURL, a core name visible inside the scope, for the module that implements the neighborhood. Never import this file at the top level of a manifest: that would put these names in the outer scope, where they belong to nobody. Each name maps to an absolute IRI under http://shex.js.org/neighborhoods/Sparql/#, shex.js's own namespace, since neighborhoods are shex.js's data-access layer rather than anything the ShEx specs define; the file declares no prefix and nothing outside the scope, so it cannot collide with another vocabulary, whatever prefix the manifest chose for it. The names outside the scope are the manifest's top-level contexts': the ShEx manifest vocabulary (https://www.w3.org/ns/shex-manifest.jsonld) and, stacked on it, shex.js's own terms (../../doc/manifest-context.jsonld), pluginURL among them (see doc/manifest-vocabulary.md for the survey this came out of). Tentative home: a SPARQL endpoint isn't specific to ShEx or to shex.js, so other RDF tooling may want this vocabulary too; if a better shared home turns up (a SPARQL-protocol-adjacent namespace, say), these IRIs should point there instead.",

  "@context": {
    "endpoint": {"@id": "http://shex.js.org/neighborhoods/Sparql/#endpoint", "@type": "@id"},
    "allOutgoing": "http://shex.js.org/neighborhoods/Sparql/#allOutgoing"
  }
}
